Parameter files for discrete logarithms in GF(p)
================================================

cado-nfs.py -dlp picks params.pNN, where NN is the number of decimal
digits of p (or the nearest available size, within 3 digits), and
params.p2ddNN for GF(p^2). The parameters that DLP shares with
factoring (polynomial selection, sieving, filtering, linear algebra)
are documented in parameters/factor/params.c90. The DLP-specific ones
are:

tasks.reconstructlog.partial
    passed as -partial to reconstructlog, which then does not
    reconstruct all the logarithms that it could.

tasks.descent.init_I, init_lim, init_lpb, init_mfb, init_ncurves,
tasks.descent.init_tkewness
    parameters of the first step of the descent of a target (see
    scripts/descent), which looks for a first relation that the rest of
    the descent can handle. init_tkewness plays the role of qmin for
    that search.

tasks.descent.I, lim0, lim1, lpb0, lpb1, mfb0, mfb1
    default sieving parameters of the subsequent descent steps, which
    the hint file (pNN.hint, see below) refines according to the size
    of the special-q. I must match the hint file, and lpb0/lpb1 are
    normally those of the sieving, so that the descent stops at primes
    whose logarithms are known.

Joux-Lercier polynomial selection
---------------------------------

The files in Joux-Lercier/ are an alternative set of parameters, for
the Joux-Lercier polynomial selection (polyselect/dlpolyselect). They
are not picked automatically; README.dlp explains how to use them.
They set:

jlpoly = true
    use dlpolyselect instead of the polynomial selection of factoring.

tasks.polyselect.degree, bound, modm
    degree is that of the polynomial with small coefficients (the other
    one has degree one less), bound bounds the size of its
    coefficients, and modm is the number of workunits the search is
    split into. bound and modm are passed to dlpolyselect, which
    requires modm to be coprime to 2*bound*(2*bound+1)*(bound+1).

fastSM = true
    passes -easySM ell to dlpolyselect, which then favours polynomials
    that make the Schirokauer maps cheap. This only pays off for small
    p; above 80 digits or so the sieving and linear algebra dominate.

tasks.sieve.sqside = 0
    the norms are larger on side 0, which is where the special-q are
    best taken.

Hint files
----------

The meaning of hint files is explained in

https://sympa.inria.fr/sympa/arc/cado-nfs/2020-04/msg00019.html

Here is the meaning of a hint file line :
21@1 1 1 I=11 20000,18,36 20000,18,36

To descend a special-q of 21 bits on side 1, use a sieve with
I=11
lim0=lim1=20000
lpb0=lpb1=18
mfb0=mfb1=36
the first block 20000,18,36 corresponding to side0 and the second to
side1.

The "1 1" in second and third positions can be safely ignored.

Each line gives therefore a rule about how to handle a node in the
descent tree.
